From e0edf4a8ee747ffd04fc7f4cd46cd1aacdf2e3dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cameron Basham <cambash23@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 21:20:50 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 110/110] glm cuda: TF_GLM_CHUNK_SOLO: a prompt step beside a
 decoding stream takes TF_GLM_CHUNK rows of the 4,096-row chunk

A port of upstream main's adaptive prompt chunk (04171ccf, #1823, option C of #1554; the docs d6e9569a, 2914b688) onto
this tree. Upstream's grid is 2,048 rows and TF_GLM_CHUNK_SOLO=4096 widens a pass to two grid chunks while no other
stream decodes, so the kept-state grid, the rings and prompt_rows stay at 2,048 and a stream decoding beside a long
prompt sees 2,048-row passes. Here patches 0094/0095 already put the grid at 4,096 (cuda_state.prefill_rows: the chunk
kept prompt states are cut on, half the index ring, the prompt buffers' rows), and a step alone is a chunk or a pair
(0050-0062). The same observable behaviour therefore comes from the other side: the grid, the rings, the buffers and
the kept-state cuts stay at 4,096, and a step beside a decoding stream takes a part of a chunk.

- TF_GLM_CHUNK_SOLO=4096 (opt-in, every rank; unset: off, every step as before) turns it on; TF_GLM_CHUNK (2048 default,
  or 1024) is the rows a busy step takes. Upstream's "2048" means its grid everywhere, which this grid cannot spell, so
  it is refused by name, as is TF_GLM_CHUNK without the switch. Both ride the ranks' fingerprint (layout 15): the other
  ranks size rank 0's busy steps from their own setting and the chunk header's flag 4.
- cuda_prompt_solo.zig decides: rank 0's busyAt (Lanes.othersDecode's word, the same predicate the pair uses) and every
  rank's allowed, with ringsAgree: busy steps over a chunk run only where they rebase the DSA index rings where the whole
  chunk does, at the chunk's start or not at all (with a 4,096 grid and an 8,192 ring that is every aligned chunk; the
  rule still guards a short ring). Off the chunk grid a step is a busy one whatever the lanes say: a chunk split into
  busy steps is finished in them, so every whole chunk and every kept state starts on the grid. Not beside prompt
  logprobs, image rows or the 2048-row profiling bucket (upstream's exclusions); a cancel part way into a chunk keeps no
  state there (cuda_native.snap_grid).
- The target MTP head (drafts without --drafter) absorbs a step's next ids in TF_GLM_CHUNK-row passes whenever the
  setting is on (prompt_solo.mtpRows, for prefillChunk and prompt_pair alike), so a whole chunk's head passes are the
  passes its busy steps make and the head's index ring rebases (State.readyMtp, at mtp_len - 16 when a pass would
  overflow it) on the same rows either way: the first GPU run of glm-solo-check showed the MTP base at 16368 against
  14320 and the MTP index-key ring laid out from it. Upstream's mtpRows is the same answer in its direction. With
  --drafter (DFlash2) the head never absorbs, so serving is untouched by this.
- No buffer grows for it (a busy step is a part of the 4,096-row buffers), so upstream's refusal beside --drafter
  (d229c4f0) has no counterpart: DFlash2 ingests each step's rows as it does a paired step's second chunk
  (dflash.promptRun keeps a step's rows from len - WIN on, whatever its width).
- Exactness: a row's result does not depend on which pass holds it (cuda_exl3's note on the common experts, the ground
  patch 0094 shipped on with identical greedy replies at 2,048 -> 4,096; glm-pair-check for 8,192 against 4,096;
  upstream's glm-solo-check for 4,096 against 2,048 on main). glm-solo-check here runs busy twins against whole-chunk
  twins: first token, next window's logits and tokens, the rings' bases, the MTP base, the marks called, the KDA records
  read next and every other state byte equal. The KDA records' stale copy and State.cur are left out as upstream leaves
  them: a prompt pass writes the other copy and flips, so twins with a different pass count differ there and nothing
  reads it (a decode window reads recAt(cur) and replays from it); the first GPU run failed on exactly that byte.

Expected: a stream decoding beside a cold ~200k prompt pauses about chunk_rows / prefill_rate a step (4,096 rows at
~2,450 tok/s: ~1.7 s of GPU time, measured 1.96-2.02 s; 2,048: ~0.85 s; 1,024: ~0.42 s), for the prompt's own cost of the
expert trellis decode amortised over fewer rows while it shares (upstream measured a 2048 -> 4096 pass at +7%). Alone,
nothing changes: whole chunks and pairs, so cold prefill and the prompt cache's boundaries stay as measured.
